@*
    The frame every part of the dashboard wears: a title, an optional line saying
    what the figures do and do not cover, a refresh control, and the part's own
    content below.

    It exists so the seven parts look like seven parts of one thing without each
    of them re-deciding what a heading is. There is nothing configurable on it —
    no fold, no hide, no reorder — because the dashboard's composition is fixed;
    the only control is refresh, which changes what is shown rather than which
    parts exist.

    Not a shared-library component. It is the dashboard's own page furniture and a
    second host would want its own rhythm, which is the same reason the storybook
    keeps sb-dashboard-panel to itself rather than publishing it.
*@

<section class="dashboard-part @CssClass" data-testid="@TestId">
    <header class="dashboard-part__header">
        <div class="dashboard-part__heading">
            <h3 class="dashboard-part__title">@Title</h3>
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Note))
            {
                <p class="dashboard-part__note" data-testid="@(TestId is null ? null : TestId + "-note")">@Note</p>
            }
        </div>

        @* A refresh while one is already in flight would only cancel and restart
           it, so the control reports the wait rather than offering to duplicate it. *@
        <IconButton AriaLabel="@($"Refresh {Title}")"
                    Title="@(Busy ? "Refreshing" : "Refresh")"
                    CssClass="btn--ghost"
                    Disabled="Busy"
                    TestId="@(TestId is null ? null : TestId + "-refresh")"
                    OnClick="() => OnRefresh.InvokeAsync()">↻</IconButton>
    </header>

    <div class="dashboard-part__content">
        @ChildContent
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    [Parameter]
    public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>One line about what the figures cover. The cost parts use it to say
    /// they are not filtered by repository, which is the one thing about this
    /// dashboard a reader could otherwise get wrong.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Note { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public bool Busy { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnRefresh { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }
}
